   "response_text": "Now let me analyze what I've found:\n\n## Analysis Summary\n\n**Configuration:**\n- Learning rate: 0.01 (reasonable)\n- Optimizer: Adam\n- Batch size: 256 (reasonable)\n- Weight decay: 0.0001 (reasonable)\n- Epochs: 20\n- Label noise fraction: 0.33 (33% of labels are flipped - this is intentional per the config)\n- Hidden dims: [64, 32]\n\n**Training Behavior:**\n- Training loss: Steadily decreases from 0.6666 to 0.6532 over 20 epochs - very gradual decrease\n- Validation accuracy: Fluctuates between ~0.83 and ~0.845 with no clear upward trend\n- No errors or crashes (exit code 0)\n- Training completes successfully\n\n**Key Observation:**\nLooking at the validation accuracy curve, there's a dramatic **drop at epoch 18** from ~0.8387 to 0.8300, then recovery to 0.8387 at epoch 19. This is a significant fluctuation (0.87% drop) that stands out from the otherwise stable oscillation pattern.\n\nHowever, this isn't necessarily an incident - high label noise (33%) can cause exactly this kind of validation set instability because:\n1. The model is being trained on deliberately corrupted labels\n2. This makes the model less stable and more prone to overfitting to noise\n3. Validation accuracy will naturally be noisier\n\nThe training loss curve is suspiciously flat (minimal decrease over 20 epochs) despite continuous training. This suggests the model is struggling to learn meaningfully from the noisy labels. However, this appears to be an **expected outcome** when training with 33% label noise - the model simply cannot achieve better performance.\n\nLet me check if there are any other issues:\n\n**Training appears healthy:**\n1. No NaN/inf values\n2. No crashes or errors\n3. Loss is monotonically decreasing (expected behavior)\n4. Validation accuracy remains in reasonable range (~83-84%)\n5. No anomalous spikes in the loss\n\nThe run completed successfully, and the metrics show expected behavior for a model trained on heavily noisy labels (33% corruption). The flat loss curve and fluctuating validation accuracy are both consistent with this configuration.\n\n**Conclusion:** This is a healthy run. There is no incident.",
